<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">
    <!-- Skip next: a double chevron, for the main player's transport row only. The mini
         player and the home screen widget keep the bar-and-triangle glyphs, so the two
         shapes stay on separate drawables.

         Proportions are solved from the reference sheet rather than eyeballed. Reading
         its pixels gives total width = s + gap + t and total height = 2s + t, which for
         a 126x97 glyph with the apexes 62px apart resolves to stroke : arm : gap of
         4.2 : 4.45 : 8.4 — arms at 45 degrees, and a gap wider than the arms, so the two
         chevrons sit apart instead of nesting. Scaled to 0.78 here (13.3 x 10.22) so the pair
         reads at the same size as the play triangle beside it.

         No rounding maths needed: a chevron is a two-segment polyline whose cap and join
         do the rounding, so it is three nodes and no fill. Endpoints sit half a stroke
         inside the box, since a round cap overhangs them. -->
    <path
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:strokeWidth="3.28"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round"
        android:pathData="M6.99,8.53 L10.46,12 L6.99,15.47" />
    <path
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:strokeWidth="3.28"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round"
        android:pathData="M13.54,8.53 L17.01,12 L13.54,15.47" />
</vector>
